# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The current implementation of the cross-chain settlement system allows for immediate changes like to validator and handler configuration without considering in-flight transactions or synchronization across different blockchains. This can lead to various issues, including unexpected transaction failures and temporarily stuck funds. This issue is present in both solidity and cairo code.

This immediate effect fails to account for:

**In-flight transactions**: Cross-chain transactions that are already in progress when the changes are made may fail unexpectedly if they no longer meet the new validation or handler requirements.
**Cross-chain synchronization**: Different blockchains may process these admin changes at different times, leading to inconsistent requirements across the network.

These issues can result in:

Unexpected transaction failures: Valid transactions initiated under previous requirements may be rejected.
Temporarily stuck funds: Assets involved in failed cross-chain transactions may become temporarily inaccessible.
Inconsistent user experience: Users may face different outcomes for similar transactions depending on timing and the specific blockchains involved.
Potential security risks: Rapid changes to validators or handlers without proper synchronization could temporarily create windows of vulnerability.

## Proof of Concept
In the SettlementSignatureVerifier contract, admin functions have immediate effects:
```solidity
function set_required_validators_num(
    uint256 _required_validators
) external virtual onlyRole(MANAGER_ROLE) {
    uint256 old = required_validators;
    required_validators = _required_validators;
    emit RequiredValidatorsChanged(msg.sender, old, required_validators);
}

function add_validator(address validator) external onlyRole(MANAGER_ROLE) {
    require(validators[validator] == false, "Validator already exists");
    validators[validator] = true;
    validator_count += 1;
    emit ValidatorAdded(msg.sender, validator);
}

function remove_validator(
    address validator
) external onlyRole(MANAGER_ROLE) {
    require(validators[validator] == true, "Validator does not exists");
    validators[validator] = false;
    validator_count -= 1;
    emit ValidatorRemoved(msg.sender, validator);
}
```

Similarly, in the ChakraSettlementHandler contract, handler validity can be immediately changed:
```solidity
function add_handler(
    string memory chain_name,
    uint256 handler
) external onlyOwner {
    handler_whitelist[chain_name][handler] = true;
}

function remove_handler(
    string memory chain_name,
    uint256 handler
) external onlyOwner {
    handler_whitelist[chain_name][handler] = false;
}
```

These changes immediately affect the validation and handling processes in the ChakraSettlement and ChakraSettlementHandler contracts:
```solidity
// In ChakraSettlement
function receive_cross_chain_msg(
    uint256 txid,
    string memory from_chain,
    uint256 from_address,
    uint256 from_handler,
    address to_handler,
    PayloadType payload_type,
    bytes calldata payload,
    uint8 sign_type,
    bytes calldata signatures
) external {
    // ... (other checks)

    require(
        signature_verifier.verify(message_hash, signatures, sign_type),
        "Invalid signature"
    );

    // ... (rest of the function)
}

// In ChakraSettlementHandler
function receive_cross_chain_msg(
    uint256 txid,
    string memory from_chain,
    uint256 from_handler,
    PayloadType payload_type,
    bytes calldata payload,
    uint8 sign_type,
    bytes calldata signatures
) external onlySettlement returns (bool) {
    if (is_valid_handler(from_chain, from_handler) == false) {
        return false;
    }
    // ... (rest of the function)
}
```
